      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "RATIONALE",
          "content": "Studies evaluating the safety of salmeterol are inconclusive, which might be the result from not taking into account the impact of concomitant inhaled corticosteroids (ICS)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To study whether salmeterol use with and without concomitant ICS, respectively, was associated with an increased risk of asthma-related hospitalizations among patients with asthma."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A case-control study nested within a cohort of patients with asthma, identified in the year 2000, over a 2-year period was conducted. Cases were subjects who had a first-time hospitalization for asthma in the year 2001, and were matched with up to five controls by age (+/- 5 years), sex, and number of asthma-related outpatient visits."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS",
          "content": "Hospitalizations and medication use were extracted from the MEDSTAT's MarketScan database."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MAIN RESULTS",
          "content": "There were 333 cases of asthma-related hospitalizations and 1607 matched control subjects. Any use of salmeterol with concomitant ICS use during the prior year was associated with a 32% risk reduction for being hospitalized due to asthma (OR = 0.68; 95% CI = 0.48, 0.96). In the presence of concomitant ICS use, patients who either used salmeterol currently or used seven or more canisters of salmeterol during the prior year had 46% (OR = 0.54, 95% CI = 0.32, 0.92), and 59% (OR = 0.41, 95% CI = 0.21, 0.79) reductions in the risk of hospital admissions for asthma, respectively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "LIMITATIONS",
          "content": "Though indirect measure of asthma severity was adjusted during the analyses, the lack of information on lung function might result in a selection bias. Additionally, only a small sample size of patients was found to use salmeterol without concomitant ICS use, and this introduced the issue of lack of power."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Use of salmeterol in conjunction with ICS is associated with a decreased risk of hospital admission for asthma."
        }
      ]},
